condition_name: Juvenile Periodontitis
condition_slug: juvenile-periodontitis
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "An uncommon disease characterized by periodontal destruction, often in the absence of overt gingival inflammation."
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: disease manifestation
    features[3]: Affects the first permanent molar and incisors,"Actinobacillus, Actinomycetes comitans has been isolated from the affected sites",Results in drifting and loss of the first permanent molar and incisors
investigations[1]: "Radiology may reveal marked bone loss interdentally, inter-radicularly and apically"
treatment:
  goals[0]:
  non_drug[8]: Control of plaque bacteria by use of antiseptic solution,Establishing a healthy gingival and periodontal attachment,Oral hygiene instruction and motivation,Regular scaling and polishing,Root planning,Splinting of mobile tooth,Periodontal surgery,"Bone regenerative techniques e.g. using polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) membranes, Bio-Oss, Bio-membrane"
  drug[7]: "Metronidazole - Adult: 200 mg orally every 8 hours for 5 days","Metronidazole - Child 1 - 3 years: 50 mg orally every 8 hours","Metronidazole - Child 3 - 7 years: 100 mg every 12 hours","Metronidazole - Child 7 - 10 years: 100 mg every 8 hours","Metronidazole - Child 10 - 18 years: 200 mg every 8 hours","Tetracycline - Adult: 250 mg orally daily for up to 21 days","Child under 12 years: metronidazole and amoxicillin (or erythromycin for those sensitive to penicillin)"
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[1]: Tetracyclines should not be given to children under 12 years
  supportive_measures[0]:
differential_diagnoses[0]:
complications[3]: Tooth loss,Malocclusion,Temporomandibular Joint (TMJ) dysfunction syndrome
prevention[0]:
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null